landing-review: vulkan-only plan, landing 11-5e-1 -- three leaf files go GL-free

Range reviewed: main...HEAD (bedd790..6ff81e2, 5 commits: 481ee81 code,
569c650 docs, 0f19c27 + 5e19276 + 6ff81e2 review fixes).
Reviewer: dedicated read-only agents, `medium` (tagpu/ddraw/** touched;
no engine or user state written, no byte patch added or moved, not
sim-adjacent).
THREE rounds: two independent reviewers on the landing, then one focused
round on the fix commits that neither of the first two ever saw.

ON THE SYNCHRONISATION CLAUSE, stated rather than waved at, because it is
the clause that would force `high`. Round 1's fix added `s_frames = 0` to
tagpu_ftime_vk_reset, which tagpu_vk.c's vk_down calls on the render
thread in ST_READY, or on the bring-up worker unwinding its own failure
in ST_STARTING. That function ALREADY wrote three plain statics from
those same call sites (s_vkN, s_vkAt, s_vkTotal); this adds a fourth of
the same kind, in the same function, from the same callers. No new
thread, no new sharing relationship, no lock, handshake, published
counter or lifetime touched -- the synchronisation is not CHANGED, it is
extended by one variable of a kind already there. The pre-existing shape
(plain statics in an instrument, reachable from two threads) was raised
by round 2 as LOW/PLAUSIBLE and is recorded in gpu-status 2.73, not fixed
here: in ST_STARTING no present has happened so no sample can be in
flight, which is an argument rather than a construction, and the worst
outcome is a report line firing one window early.

WHAT IT DOES. Deletes the GL half of tagpu_ftime.c, tagpu_overlay.c and
tagpu_text.c plus two forwarding functions in tagpu_mark.c and
tagpu_text.c: 13 functions, 34 file-scope statics, 52 of the tree's 592
masked GL call sites, nine GL-bearing files down to six. Its real output
is a structural find: tagpu_overlay.c polled wglGetCurrentContext once a
frame and ran six modules' *_glreset on a change; oglu_load_dll() -- the
only code that resolves wglCreateContext / wglMakeCurrent -- has no
caller, so the handle is always 0 and the branch had no first iteration.
Deleting it orphaned sixteen *_glreset functions across sixteen files.
Only the two whose whole body was a forward are deleted; the other
fourteen are left standing and labelled, because their modules still hold
live GL state behind escalation reason 1.

THE COROLLARY THIS LANDING PAID FOR (11-5e-1, now in the plan): a
predicate can be pinned by the ABSENCE of a thing rather than by a value,
and when it is, the root of a dead TREE is that predicate and not any
function in the tree. So the search is "which tests can never be true",
not "which functions have no callers" -- the latter finds leaves. And
when a predicate is pinned by an absence, write down the missing CALLER,
never the missing name: a name scan proves a spelling, not an absence.

ROUND 1 (reachability + per-function deletions) -- 11 findings, no
runtime defect. Verified the pin four independent ways (no caller of
oglu_load_dll, no invocation of the wgl pointers, no GL renderer arm in
dd.c, no opengl32 import in the game binary), confirmed all 13 deletions
callerless and absent from the built DLL, confirmed the s_dirty/s_gdirty
removal lossless (every raster path that set a flag also bumped the
serial), and reproduced 592->540 per file.
  MEDIUM-1 was the landing's own central sentence: "wglCreateContext ...
  appear in no source of this build" is FALSE -- they are at
  opengl_utils.c:107 and :110, string literals behind an x prefix. The
  conclusion stands; the invariant is restated as the missing caller.
  MEDIUM-2: the cascade diagram quoted MASKED line numbers, in the
  section that documents that very bug. MEDIUM-3: render_ogl.c died in
  11-2, not 4d-1. MEDIUM-4: the tombstone pointed at `tacli glshot`, a
  retired stub. MEDIUM-5: tagpu_ftime.h's head was still present-tense
  about deleted code. LOW 6-11 fixed; LOW-11 (s_state a constant with a
  dead guard) taken as a finding and collapsed to a one-shot.

ROUND 2 (changed-but-live code + the measurement) -- 16 findings, no
runtime defect.
  HIGH: the only live code this landing changed sits behind
  tagpu_ftime.on, which the fixture never armed -- and a pixel diff is
  structurally incapable of covering an instrument that writes to
  tagpu.log. Armed and re-run: all three changed functions exercised,
  report() prints, the toggle clears the ring.
  MEDIUM-1b: tagpu_ftime_poll's comment DENIED a pool that exists
  (tagpu_vk.c's tsPool / tsPend[MAXIMG]; the write gated on
  tagpu_ftime_armed(), the drain not gated). Corrected, with the residual
  window named and its by-construction fix (an arm-generation identity)
  left to tagpu_vk.c's seam, where the pool lives.
  Also: sixteen FILES not ten; "labelled in place" was false for four of
  five; two comments asserting the opposite of the truth; nm cannot see a
  removed CALL; five dirty-flag assignments not four;
  tagpu_hires_draw_frame does not exist.

ROUND 3 (the two fix commits alone, 569c650..5e19276) -- 7 findings, and
ALL FOUR live-code areas came back clean: the s_state branch is provably
unreachable on main too, no tagpu_zoom_frame_end() exit was lost in the
collapse, s_frames parity with the toggle path is exact, the banners are
attached to the right functions, and both string substitutions matched
once. The seven were documentation, every one verified against the source
before it was applied:
  MEDIUM: nine cascade line numbers one too high, because round 1's own
  #include removal shifted tagpu_native.c below :95 -- in the section
  that exists to correct line numbers, which had already compensated for
  that shift in one place and not in the diagram. A line number is a fact
  with a version.
  MEDIUM: the residual-hole window understated two to three times over.
  The slot index is `s_vk.frame % s_vk.nimg`, not MAXIMG, so it is six to
  eight frames a second at three or four images and sixteen at the clamp,
  not "about five". The bound is not the value.
  MEDIUM: tagpu_scaffold.c's banner borrowed tagpu_native.c's
  justification -- that module has ZERO GL sites and is not held by the
  escalation at all.
  LOW: the tsPend clearers misnamed; `n=256/300` shows the counter
  restarted and NOT that nothing leaked (a stale pair is one of the 300);
  "dd.c has exactly two arms" is four arms to two destinations; and the
  minimap rect's 126 is engine geometry while its 128 is the panel, so
  "0 px outside" carries ~22 columns of slack.

VERIFIED BY RUNNING IT. one-unit, Two Continents, 1024x768,
renderer=vulkan confirmed in the ini after the run, the DLL md5-matched
into the gamedir, both builds settled to `vk: census: 6 pass(es) drew`.
  Cross-build: 44 differing pixels of 786432, 0 outside the minimap rect
  [0:126,0:128] -- the 44 are minimap blips for off-screen units the two
  runs' AI built in different numbers (alive=14 against alive=9,
  onscreen=1 in both).
  Same-build control: 0 px.
  The review rounds' own fixes re-measured: 48 px, 0 outside.
  Instrument: ftime: vk p50 0.383 ms p99 0.620 ms (n=256/300), and /300
  again after an off/on toggle.

TWO METHOD DEFECTS THIS LANDING FOUND IN ITSELF, both now in ta-drive:
  1. The comment-masking helper blanked comment NEWLINES, so masked line
     numbers were not source line numbers (1621 lines out on
     tagpu_native.c). Counts and caller answers were unaffected; five of
     seven quoted line numbers in 11-5d's depth-twin chain were wrong and
     are corrected.
  2. A same-build control at 0 px proves the scene is STATIC, not
     SETTLED. A stable 3-pass state is greyscale and differs from a
     6-pass capture by 202695 pixels -- the same gap, dressed as a
     regression. Both gates are now required: three captures at 0 px, and
     `6 pass(es) drew` in the census.

NOT COVERED. tagpu_shadow.c and tagpu_hires_draw.c (escalation reason 1)
and, since this landing found the dependency, opengl_utils.c with them --
it defines the GL entry points those two call. The fixture did not arm
tagpu_fps.on, did not reach the string-atlas raster path (its only
remaining consumer is tagpu_mark_emit_text, which needs a squad-tagged
unit), and covered neither the shell, a second resolution, renderer=gdi,
nor a display-mode change -- the one event the deleted watch existed for.
